# %% [markdown] id="jK3xGZek9np-"
# ### Precision
#
#
# **Precision** can be defined as the percentage of correctly predicted positive outcomes out of all the predicted positive outcomes. It can be given as the ratio of true positives (TP) to the sum of true and false positives (TP + FP).
#
#
# So, **Precision** identifies the proportion of correctly predicted positive outcome. It is more concerned with the positive class than the negative class.
#
#
#
# Mathematically, precision can be defined as the ratio of `TP to (TP + FP)`.
#
#

# %% id="36Mr703p9np-" outputId="3624439e-a088-4484-f3cc-7209fce1f62c" colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} executionInfo={"status": "ok", "timestamp": 1725352357595, "user_tz": -180, "elapsed": 32, "user": {"displayName": "Ahmed Yousry", "userId": "15736517196575255940"}}
# print precision score

precision = TP / float(TP + FP)


print('Precision : {0:0.4f}'.format(precision))


# %% [markdown] id="CnDQz74n9np-"
# ### Recall
#
#
# Recall can be defined as the percentage of correctly predicted positive outcomes out of all the actual positive outcomes.
# It can be given as the ratio of true positives (TP) to the sum of true positives and false negatives (TP + FN). **Recall** is also called **Sensitivity**.
#
#
# **Recall** identifies the proportion of correctly predicted actual positives.
#
#
# Mathematically, recall can be given as the ratio of `TP to (TP + FN)`.
#
#

# %% id="bwvCKu7D9np-" outputId="c9446d0e-a1e8-4e66-d84d-91272fe9b7c0" colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} executionInfo={"status": "ok", "timestamp": 1725352357595, "user_tz": -180, "elapsed": 31, "user": {"displayName": "Ahmed Yousry", "userId": "15736517196575255940"}}
recall = TP / float(TP + FN)

print('Recall or Sensitivity : {0:0.4f}'.format(recall))

# %% [markdown] id="FYNqRK-E9np-"
# ### True Positive Rate
#
#
# **True Positive Rate** is synonymous with **Recall**.
#

# %% id="ktjB58nm9np_" outputId="b305aab1-53c1-4f7b-8c85-283b0d482d77" colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} executionInfo={"status": "ok", "timestamp": 1725352357595, "user_tz": -180, "elapsed": 28, "user": {"displayName": "Ahmed Yousry", "userId": "15736517196575255940"}}
true_positive_rate = TP / float(TP + FN)


print('True Positive Rate : {0:0.4f}'.format(true_positive_rate))

# %% [markdown] id="V-6Ig-HD9np_"
# ### False Positive Rate

# %% id="tLjX4HbT9np_" outputId="7d5247bc-efad-4653-c1af-50a357709ed3" colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} executionInfo={"status": "ok", "timestamp": 1725352357595, "user_tz": -180, "elapsed": 27, "user": {"displayName": "Ahmed Yousry", "userId": "15736517196575255940"}}
false_positive_rate = FP / float(FP + TN)


print('False Positive Rate : {0:0.4f}'.format(false_positive_rate))

# %% [markdown] id="2JQqpa6I9np_"
# ### Specificity

# %% id="bs1MZrSy9np_" outputId="af7146cc-f583-4820-e90b-82aa796a6b65" colab={"base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"} executionInfo={"status": "ok", "timestamp": 1725352357595, "user_tz": -180, "elapsed": 26, "user": {"displayName": "Ahmed Yousry", "userId": "15736517196575255940"}}
specificity = TN / (TN + FP)

print('Specificity : {0:0.4f}'.format(specificity))

# %% [markdown] id="pP0ijyA89np_"
# ### f1-score
#
#
# **f1-score** is the weighted harmonic mean of precision and recall. The best possible **f1-score** would be 1.0 and the worst
# would be 0.0.  **f1-score** is the harmonic mean of precision and recall. So, **f1-score** is always lower than accuracy measures as they embed precision and recall into their computation. The weighted average of `f1-score` should be used to
# compare classifier models, not global accuracy.
#
#
#

